I wouldn't say it's quite a niche scenario, more like a more advanced scenario for more complex use.

Composing the settings of each sink with a mix of reading the appsettings file and further configuration of properties that are not possible to configure from a json (because they are not primitive types deserializable from json like a Func<>, Action<>, etc) or that you want to override under certain more complex conditions other than appsettings.{EnvironmentName}.json which is what the base Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration system supports.

It is true that the problem is in the context of this `Serilog.Settings.Configuration` package but I really do not know if it is possible to solve it without touching anything of the core of Serilog and that is why I have created the issue in this repository.

One option is the API design shown by @sungam3r.

Another option that would only affect the Serilog configuration package could be something like this (if it is possible to do it without touching anything in the Serilog core package which I don't know either):

Add this property in the following class:

```csharp
public sealed class ConfigurationReaderOptions
{
    public Action<ILogEventSink>? OnSinkCreated { get; init; }
}
```

Usage:

```csharp
var logger = new LoggerConfiguration()
    .ReadFrom.Configuration(configuration, new ConfigurationReaderOptions {
        OnSinkCreated = sink => {
            if (sink is GraylogSink graylogSink) {
                // Change sink configuration if possible
            }
        }
    )
    .CreateLogger();
```

I think by its design and intention, `SelfLog` should stay ultra-simple. The goals for `SelfLog` are to provide a debugging mechanism when things go wrong, rather than a general monitoring mechanism.

If we decide to invest more energy here, perhaps we'd be better off approaching this with a clean slate?

E.g. if the real goal is to detect logging failures, perhaps a general fallback mechanism would be a better fit, and could be useful without relying on "fixing" all of the historical (mis-)uses of `SelfLog`?

Purely an illustrative sketch:

```csharp
enum LoggingFailureKind
{
    // Events will be retried
    Temporary,
    // Not retried
    Permanent,
    // Sink or logger is going offline for good
    Final
}

interface ILoggingFailureListener
{
    Task OnLoggingFailedAsync(
        LoggingFailureKind kind,
        string message,
        SinkId? sink,
        IEnumerable<LogEvent>? events,
        Exception? exception);
}
```

Serilog would automatically write these to `SelfLog` so that it remains useful in its current role. More sophisticated use cases like failover sinks or alerting could subscribe directly.

Food for thought, anyway!

I hit an interesting issue relating to this in an async background service that was supposed to be handling google pub sub messages so I don't know if the threading aspect has any impact. The code looked something like this:

```
private async Task<SubscriberClient.Reply> HandleMessage(PubsubMessage message, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    using var context =_logger.BeginScope(new Dictionary<string, object>
    {
                { LogScopeMessageId, message.MessageId }
    });
    
    ... <-- Last line debugger his before exception is thrown
    
    // MessageHandler is a defined in another scope outside of this call. I am able to use that successfully
    // in other log message formats without supplying it as a format argument 
    _logger.LogDebug("Handler {MessageHandler} received {@Message}", message); <-- Debugger never hits this line
    try
    {
        ...
    }
    catch (Exception e)
    {
        _logger.LogError(e, "Error handling {@Message}", message); <-- Debugger never hits this line
        return SubscriberClient.Reply.Nack; <-- App breaks on this line
    }
}
```

When picking up messages of the queue nothing is ever logged, not even warnings about parameters. But if I removed the `{MessageHandler}` placeholder everything started working as expected. As you can see it is interested that I cannot hit the last log message outside the try catch or the one in the catch. How does it even get in the catch? My assumption would be it has nothing to do with serilog but as soon as I removed the placeholder it works. And the reason I am posting in here as opposed to a new ticket, I didn't get any diagnostic self logs.
